    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a laboratory container always need a building permit (Baugenehmigung)?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Not always. In many German Länder, temporary siting of up to 3 months is permit-free. For permanent use beyond 3 months a building permit (Baugenehmigung) or at least a building notification (Bauanzeige) is generally required. What is decisive are the Land building code (Landesbauordnung), the type of use and the location." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What additional permits does a laboratory container with hazardous substances need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "In addition to the building permit, further permits may be required: an immission-control permit under the Federal Immission Control Act (BImSchG) at certain substance quantities, a water-law authorisation (wasserrechtliche Erlaubnis) for laboratory wastewater containing hazardous substances, and a notification under the Biological Agents Ordinance (BioStoffV) for BSL-2 or BSL-3 laboratories." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does the building permit for a laboratory container take?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "In the simplified procedure 4 to 8 weeks, in the regular building-permit procedure 8 to 16 weeks. For additional permits (immission control, water law) a further 4 to 12 weeks may be added. A preliminary building enquiry (Bauvoranfrage) in advance can accelerate the process." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a Bauvoranfrage and a Bauantrag?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The Bauvoranfrage (preliminary building enquiry) clarifies in advance whether a project is in principle capable of being permitted – for example with regard to location, type of use and development plan (Bebauungsplan). It is legally binding, but is not a substitute for the Bauantrag (building application). The Bauantrag is the formal permit application with complete documentation." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What documents do I need for the Bauantrag of a laboratory container?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Typical documents: Bauantrag form, site plan (1:500), floor plans and sections, building description with statement of use, structural proof, fire-protection concept, drainage plan and, where applicable, a hazardous-substances concept. For laboratories a ventilation concept and a hygiene plan are often added." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can Planexus support the permitting of a laboratory container?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Planexus accompanies the entire permitting process: from review of the building-law situation via preparation of the application documents through to coordination with the authorities. As a specialist for laboratory containers we know the typical pitfalls and can accelerate the process considerably." }
        }
      ]
    })} />

